  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function on [0,2] that minimizes the maximum correlation integral C5, which represents the worst-case overlap with shifted versions of itself. Our approach uses a combination of multi-resolution optimization and simulated annealing with geometric constraints. We represent the solution as a piecewise constant function and optimize by:\n   - Starting with a coarse grid (n=64)\n   - Doubling the grid size (128, 256, 512 points)\n   - Using interpolated parent solutions to transfer information between grids\n   - Applying constrained simulated annealing with geometric preservation\n   - Maintaining integral=1 by projection\nKey improvements over previous methods:\n   - More effective temperature schedule based on time budget\n   - Geometric preservation in stochastic moves\n   - Fresher candidate distribution with adaptive local search\n   - Explicit constraint satisfaction through projection\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nThe problem minimizes:\n   C5 = max_k \u222b h(t)(1-h(t+k)) dt\nSubject to:\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n   \u222b h(x) dx = 1\nDiscrete representation:\n   h(x) represented on n points equally spaced in [0,2]\n   \u222b h(x) dx approximated by sum(h)*dx where dx=2/n\n   Correlation implemented via np.correlate(mode='full') and scaling\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Initialization: Use provided initial_h_values or generate Thue-Morse pattern\n   - Grid management: Double grid size periodically (64->128->256->512)\n   - Projection: Use Euclidean projection to satisfy 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum= n/2\n   - Optimization: Use simulated annealing with:\n        \u2022 Geometric move preservation\n        \u2022 Adaptive cooling schedule\n        \u2022 Integral maintenance\n        \u2022 Local search improvements\n   - Evaluation: Compute C5 using np.correlate and scaling\n   - Termination: Return best candidate when time budget expires\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Temperature schedule: \n        start_temp=0.004 * start_time^(1/3)\n        decay_schedule = (start_temp)^2 * time_remaining\n   - Grid sequence:\n        [64, 128, 256, 512] with time allocation [0.15, 0.25, 0.3, 0.4] * total_budget\n   - Move types:\n        Basic: h[i]-d, h[j]+d \n        Geometric: preserves pre-image structure\n        Local search: For 5% of iterations, convert the best candidate to random balanced binary representation and re-optimize briefly\n\n5. Numerical robustness:\n   - All operations use exact projection to maintain feasibility\n   - Time management tracks wall-clock time\n   - Candidates are clipped to [0,1] after perturbation\n   - Final solution projected as safeguard\n   - Maximum iteration count limited to 10^6 * population_size\n\n6. Runtime safeguards:\n   - Time_left() function adjusts temperature decay\n   - Timed experiment recorded in previous improved program\n   - Scoring functions exactly match provided evaluator\n   - Best candidate tracked across grid sizes\n   - Fallback: Valid candidate preserved through projection",
